WEBVTT

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:00.730
<b>Host
Sakai Masato</b>

00:00:00.730 --> 00:00:04.850
In the Taisho era, there was one young author who was renowned as a prodigy

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:04.940
for his elegant writing style and articulately composed stories.

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:08.070
Prodigy

00:00:08.270 --> 00:00:09.320
That was Akutagawa Ryunosuke.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:11.610
<b>Akutagawa Ryunosuke</b>

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:13.240
At the young age of 23, he published <i>The Nose</i>.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:14.530
The Nose

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:14.740
At the young age of 23, he published <i>The Nose</i>.

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:18.740
Upon reading the work, Natsume Soseki
wrote the following in his letter to Akutagawa:

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:23.710
"I found your story very interesting. I admire your work.

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:26.540
"You will be a unique writer in the literary world."

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:27.420
As a friend, Kikuchi Kan
acclaimed Akutagawa's works

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:29.630
<b>Kikuchi Kan</b>

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:29.840
Akutagawa Prize

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:31.630
and established the Akutagawa Prize.
<b>Kikuchi Kan</b>

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:34.970
It's still seen as a gateway to
success for many young authors.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:39.430
It's said that even Dazai Osamu
showed admiration for Akutagawa.

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:43.520
The work this renowned prodigy
published when he was twenty-seven

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:43.560
is this book, <i>The Spider's Thread</i>.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.600
The Spider's Thread
Akutagawa's first children's book, published in 1918

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.730
It was his first literary work aimed at children.

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:49.440
Akutagawa's first children's book, published in 1918
The Spider's Thread

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:49.610
It was highly praised as a noticeably prominent work even among the Taisho fairy tales.
